tracks.summary:
Schema comparison and vector tests, HCP 2.0-draft-3 (b790561) against the proposed hcp/* schemas. The schemas are identical on #1380, #1381 and #1382.

Counts. 59 pairings: the PR's 55 plus 4 of mine (hard-delete response, three grants/request parts), checked one by one. 422 node pairs, 339 differences. All 38 of the PR's allowlisted differences reproduce. The other 301 are outside its list because the PR's drift check, by design, does not compare bounds, patterns, formats, closed payloads or conditionals (hcp-drift.mjs:23-27). It is also not in CI. The 301 classify as:
- 12 redundant;
- 90 intentional and documented;
- 88 intentional but undocumented (upper size caps only);
- 111 change meaning: 47 documented, 64 not. The 64 are 56 non-empty-string rules, 4 merged unions, 2 dropped formats, and 2 closed request roots.

Vectors:
- HCP → TT: 22 cases from 15 HCP examples. 19 pass. 3 fail, all explained: Q42 `directives`, the Q11 hard-delete shape, and an HCP placeholder that fails HCP's own schema.
- TT → HCP: 72 documents (demo transcript and spec.md examples). All 72 pass their TT schema. Against HCP, 38 pass, 1 fails (hard-delete has no `status`, Q11), and 33 have no HCP schema to check against.
- Edge: 40 vectors plus 26 string probes. HCP and TT disagree on 20 of the 40 and on 7 of the 26.

Nothing blocks interop. Four things to ask Brendan:
- create and revise refuse top-level custom entry members, which HCP 4.2 says a provider MUST keep;
- #1381 misses the disclosed-credential rule on query results;
- four URI members drop `format: uri`;
- 144 bounds are undocumented.

For Keyring:
- passing a TT schema does not mean an object is valid HCP. HCP's own schemas accept the TT shapes, so we can run them as a second check;
- `directives` can be null;
- our validator has to load the schemas from files or bundles, not by their published `$id`;
- HCP's own MCP tools take a different argument shape for create and revise than the TT payloads.

findings.corrected:
Severity stays "ok" for the spec text, plus one "note" about the libraries. All 28 hcp/* specs declare targetFrameworkVersion "0.6.0". The pin's SPEC.md already carries "### B.1 Framework version 0.7.0" at line 2016. specs/_framework (0.1 to 0.6, including 0.6/trust-task.schema.json) is byte-identical between the pin 2a66a0351 and the PR branch. The SPEC.md diff from the pin to the PR does not change any rule for envelope members, proofs, cryptosuites, proof purpose or digests (§4.2, §4.7, §4.7.1, §4.7.3, §4.9.3).

What the diff does change:
- the generated-from source sha in the header;
- ceremony wording in the glossary, the §4.2 `ceremony` row, §4.11 and §6.7;
- a new "Verdict" glossary term;
- the new §4.13, whose normative §4.13.3 includes rule 5: a held document's expiresAt and timestamp window (consumer items 4 and 13) are not applied again on a return to Gather;
- the new producer MUST at SPEC.md:1122;
- a real change in §8.6, not just a restatement: a decline is now evidence and the policy is evaluated again, a decision task reports its refusal in the success response, and an abandoned hold ends `cancelled`.

The sentence "the PR does not touch the proof libraries" is true only of the PR's own diff against origin/main. Between our pin (2026-11-10, #1079) and the PR branch, the proof libraries changed a lot: trust-tasks-proof, trust-tasks-ts-proof and trust-tasks-dart-proof, 40 files, +4036/-127. The verifiers changed behaviour:
- #1168: proofPurpose limited to the four signing relationships, `expires` checked, strict RFC 8032 Ed25519, small-order keys refused;
- #1233: the proof's @context must equal the document's;
- #1210: out-of-range timestamps refused;
- #1167: bounded multibase decoding;
- #1204: client response proofs verified as received.

These enforce rules SPEC.md already stated, so no spec rule changed. But the pin's libraries are stale as a byte-level oracle for how the PR's demos verify proofs. Keyring's own documentProof.ts defaults to assertionMethod, which is allowed, and copies @context only when carryContext is set. So it is likely compatible, but that is UNVERIFIED against the new shared vectors. So "no pin bump for envelope or proof reasons" holds for the spec. It does not hold if the pinned libraries are used as the reference implementation.
